WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.960
So far you have read logs with docker compose logs.

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.412
That works on one machine, while the containers exist.

3
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:12.526
Remove a container, and its logs go with it.

4
00:00:12.526 --> 00:00:15.748
With ten servers, you would search in ten places.

5
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:21.800
Loki is Grafana Labs' log store: one place for the logs of every container.

6
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:24.461
Loki does not scrape like Prometheus.

7
00:00:24.461 --> 00:00:28.332
A log is a stream of events, with no current value to read.

8
00:00:28.332 --> 00:00:31.349
So a collector sends the lines as they appear.

9
00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:33.580
In the lab, that is Grafana Alloy.

10
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.692
Alloy gives each line a few labels, such as service web, and the container name.

11
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:42.434
All lines with the same labels form one log stream.

12
00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:43.820
Here, three streams.

13
00:00:44.633 --> 00:00:46.152
Here is the main idea.

14
00:00:46.152 --> 00:00:48.153
Loki indexes only the labels.

15
00:00:48.153 --> 00:00:53.123
The text of each line is compressed into chunks, and not indexed at all.

16
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:56.146
Elasticsearch indexes every word instead.

17
00:00:56.146 --> 00:01:01.840
That costs more disk and memory, and in return finds a word across all logs faster.

18
00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:03.487
Loki stays cheap to run.

19
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:08.013
A LogQL query starts with a stream selector in braces.

20
00:01:08.013 --> 00:01:12.391
Loki adds a service_name label by itself, copied from service.

21
00:01:12.391 --> 00:01:14.439
So this picks the web stream.

22
00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:16.629
The labels picked one stream.

23
00:01:16.629 --> 00:01:18.456
Only its text gets scanned.

24
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:23.381
Inside that stream, the lines are plain text.

25
00:01:23.381 --> 00:01:25.927
The query's pipeline scans just these.

26
00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:27.884
Step one, a line filter.

27
00:01:27.884 --> 00:01:31.822
!= "/healthz" drops every line that contains /healthz.

28
00:01:31.822 --> 00:01:33.791
The health checks are gone.

29
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.119
Step two, | json.

30
00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:44.548
It parses each JSON line, so method, path, status and duration_ms become labels, for this query only.

31
00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:49.831
Step three, a filter on a field: status greater than or equal to 500.

32
00:01:49.831 --> 00:01:52.033
Only the failed requests stay.

33
00:01:52.867 --> 00:01:57.310
Step four, line_format, rewrites each line from its fields.

34
00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.398
Now each one reads GET slash, arrow, 500.

35
00:02:00.398 --> 00:02:01.377
Easy to scan.

36
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.832
Put line filters before the parser.

37
00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:10.275
A plain text filter is the cheapest step, and every line it drops is a line the parser never touches.

38
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.622
One last rule, and it matters most.

39
00:02:13.622 --> 00:02:15.629
Here is the web stream again.

40
00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:18.563
Every new label value makes a new stream.

41
00:02:18.563 --> 00:02:23.356
Put a user ID in a label, and one stream becomes thousands of tiny ones.

42
00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:27.307
Loki slows down, and fills its index with tiny chunks.

43
00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:33.092
So keep labels few: service, environment, cluster, container.

44
00:02:33.092 --> 00:02:36.521
Never a request ID, a user ID, or an IP address.

45
00:02:36.521 --> 00:02:40.593
Keep those in the line itself, where a filter finds them.

46
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.180
In Loki, labels pick the streams, and a pipeline scans just their text.

47
00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:49.008
Keep labels few, and the text stays cheap.
